WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.849
An inequality describes a set of allowed inputs rather than necessarily one solution

00:00:05.849 --> 00:00:14.233
If x plus two must stay below five, subtracting two from both sides shows that every input below three works.

00:00:15.533 --> 00:00:17.652
Including equality changes the boundary

00:00:17.652 --> 00:00:24.433
When the condition says at most five, input three is allowed because it produces exactly five

00:00:24.433 --> 00:00:30.367
A filled boundary point includes that input, whereas an open boundary point excludes it.

00:00:31.667 --> 00:00:35.688
Multiplying or dividing by a negative number reverses order

00:00:35.688 --> 00:00:42.838
Dividing negative two x less than six by negative two gives x greater than negative three

00:00:42.838 --> 00:00:48.200
Reflection across zero explains why the direction of the comparison must switch.

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:52.693
Always test a value from the proposed interval

00:00:52.693 --> 00:00:59.877
Zero is greater than negative three, and its original left side is zero, which is less than six

00:00:59.877 --> 00:01:04.667
Checking a value outside the interval also helps detect a reversed inequality.
